#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# check-conformance-drift — which cited tests have MOVED since the last sweep.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (K-1123). `bin/check-conformance-anchors` proves every anchor
# the conformance matrix cites still RESOLVES — the named `it`/`describe`/
# `context` label exists, exactly once, in the file beside it. That is a check on
# the CITATION. It says nothing about the code the citation points at: a cited
# example can be rewritten line by line, keep its label, and go on resolving
# while no longer asserting the statement the matrix claims it covers.
#
# So the matrix is walked instead — by a human, or an agent with a finite budget,
# against a work-list of ~185 rows with no change-detection on it. Predictably
# that samples: the last sweep hand-verified about ten rows, chosen by section
# recency rather than by what had changed, while 257 commits had landed since the
# matrix was rebuilt. Nothing was wrong with the judgement; there was simply no
# way to ask which rows deserved it.
#
# Whether an anchored region still ASSERTS its row is a judgement no script can
# make. Whether it CHANGED is mechanical. This script is the second question and
# only the second question: it fingerprints each anchored region when a sweep
# records a baseline, and afterwards lists the anchors — and, given the matrix,
# the rows — whose region no longer matches. The next sweep re-judges those and
# can say so with a number instead of a sample.
#
# WHAT IS FINGERPRINTED, AND WHY NOT SOMETHING SIMPLER.
#   * NOT the file's blob sha. One line moved in a 500-line spec would flag every
#     anchor in it; `well_known_spec.rb` alone carries 33.
#   * NOT the anchor line alone. That changes only when the label changes, which
#     is precisely the event `check-conformance-anchors` already fails on — the
#     fingerprint would duplicate an existing gate and detect nothing new.
#   * THE ANCHORED REGION: the line the anchor sits on, plus every following line
#     indented MORE deeply than it (blank lines are carried but do not end the
#     region; trailing blanks are trimmed). That is the block the label opens,
#     under both shapes this repo cites — RSpec's `it "…" do … end`, where the
#     body is indented and the closing `end` sits back at the label's column, and
#     `e2e/assistant.sh`'s `assert "…" \` with its indented continuation lines.
#     It is the code the citation offers as evidence, which is the thing whose
#     change should provoke a re-read.
#   * AND NO PADDING FLOOR, which was tried first and MEASURED WRONG (2026-08-27,
#     at the first baseline): 61 of the 537 anchors open nothing and so
#     fingerprint a single line. Extending those to a fixed 3-line window looked
#     like the safe bias until the 61 were actually read:
#     51 are `e2e/assistant.sh` one-line asserts whose line already carries the
#     whole assertion INCLUDING the expected value (the anchor is only the label,
#     so a changed expectation moves the fingerprint), and the file packs 82
#     anchors two lines apart — so the padding would have coupled each assert to
#     its neighbours and flagged three rows for every one that changed. The rule
#     stays "the block the label opens", and where that is one line, one line is
#     what is fingerprinted.
# The digest is the first 16 hex of SHA-256 over the region's exact bytes. This
# detects change; it is not a tamper control and nothing here pretends otherwise.
#
# WHY CONTENT AND NOT `git diff <last-sweep-sha>..HEAD`. Content answers in a
# DIRTY tree, which is where a fix wave lives, and it does not depend on commit
# identity — this repository's history has been rewritten once already, and every
# recorded sha with it. A baseline of bytes survives that; a baseline of shas
# does not.
#
# IT IS NOT A GATE, AND MUST NOT BECOME ONE. Drift here is the NORMAL state
# between sweeps: every legitimate refactor of a cited test produces it. A CI job
# failing on it would have to be silenced immediately, and silencing it means
# re-recording the baseline in the same commit — which sets the interval to zero
# and destroys the only signal the tool has. The baseline must advance when a
# SWEEP has re-judged the rows, never when a commit lands. Hence exit 2 (drift,
# triage it) is distinct from exit 1 (cannot answer), and no workflow runs this.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER, stated plainly because an over-claimed guard is worse
# than none:
#   * IT SEES BYTES, NOT MEANING. A cited example can keep every byte and stop
#     asserting its row because something it CALLS changed — a shared helper, a
#     `let`, a factory, the implementation under test. Those are invisible here.
#     A clean run is not evidence the matrix is true; it is evidence about where
#     the text moved.
#   * IT SEES THE REGION, NOT THE FILE. Moving a `before` hook, a `let` or a
#     `subject` OUTSIDE the anchored block changes the example's behaviour and
#     not its fingerprint. The sharpest case of this is an anchor that names a
#     COMMENT whose subject is the code BELOW it at the same indentation: the
#     region is then the comment alone and the code it describes is invisible.
#     Measured at the first baseline: three anchors of 537 fingerprint a comment
#     line and nothing else.
#   * IT OVER-REPORTS ON PURPOSE. A re-indentation or a comment tweak inside the
#     region reads as drift. For a triage tool that is the right bias: a false
#     positive costs a glance, a false negative costs a certified stale row.
#   * IT INHERITS THE MANIFEST'S SCOPE. Only anchors exported to
#     `spec/conformance-anchors.tsv` are covered — citations into other repos are
#     not in it and are not seen here.
#
# Usage (stdlib only, no bundle, no database, no network):
#   bin/check-conformance-drift                     # report drift against the baseline
#   bin/check-conformance-drift --matrix PATH       # …naming the matrix rows affected
#   bin/check-conformance-drift --record            # re-record the baseline at the current tree
#   bin/check-conformance-drift --record --label T  # …with a free-text label in its header
#
# Exit codes, and they are three on purpose:
#   0 — every anchored region matches the baseline
#   2 — some moved: the list is the next sweep's D1 work-list, not a failure
#   1 — the question could not be answered (missing manifest/baseline/file)

require "digest"
require "shellwords"

ROOT     = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)
MANIFEST = File.join(ROOT, "spec", "conformance-anchors.tsv")
BASELINE = File.join(ROOT, "spec", "conformance-drift-baseline.tsv")

USAGE = <<~TEXT
  usage: bin/check-conformance-drift [--matrix PATH] [--record [--label TEXT]]
         0 = no drift, 2 = regions moved (triage), 1 = cannot answer
TEXT

record = false
matrix = nil
label  = nil

argv = ARGV.dup
until argv.empty?
  arg = argv.shift
  case arg
  when "--record" then record = true
  when "--matrix" then matrix = argv.shift
  when "--label"  then label  = argv.shift
  when "-h", "--help"
    puts USAGE
    exit 0
  else
    warn "check-conformance-drift: unknown argument #{arg.inspect}"
    warn USAGE
    exit 1
  end
end

if matrix && !File.file?(matrix)
  warn "check-conformance-drift: --matrix #{matrix}: no such file"
  exit 1
end

unless File.file?(MANIFEST)
  warn "check-conformance-drift: missing #{MANIFEST} — it is generated with the matrix"
  exit 1
end

# ── the manifest: the same path/anchor pairs bin/check-conformance-anchors gates
pairs = File.readlines(MANIFEST, chomp: true).filter_map do |line|
  next if line.start_with?("#") || line.strip.empty?

  rel, anchor = line.split("\t", 2)
  next if rel.nil? || anchor.nil? || anchor.empty?

  [rel, anchor]
end

if pairs.empty?
  warn "check-conformance-drift: #{MANIFEST} lists no anchors — nothing to fingerprint"
  exit 1
end

# ── the anchored region ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
@bodies = {}

def body_of(rel)
  @bodies.fetch(rel) do
    path = File.join(ROOT, rel)
    @bodies[rel] = File.file?(path) ? File.readlines(path, chomp: true) : nil
  end
end

def indent_of(line)
  line[/\A[ \t]*/].length
end

# The line the anchor sits on, plus every following line indented more deeply
# than it. Blank lines ride along but never end the region, and trailing blanks
# are trimmed by only ever advancing `last` onto a non-blank line.
def region_at(body, idx)
  base = indent_of(body[idx])
  last = idx
  j    = idx + 1
  while j < body.length
    line = body[j]
    unless line.strip.empty?
      break if indent_of(line) <= base

      last = j
    end
    j += 1
  end
  body[idx..last]
end

def fingerprint(lines)
  Digest::SHA256.hexdigest(lines.join("\n"))[0, 16]
end

# `nil` fingerprint = the region could not be taken; the reason rides with it.
def measure(rel, anchor)
  body = body_of(rel)
  return [nil, "file is gone"] if body.nil?

  hits = body.each_index.select { |i| body[i].include?(anchor) }
  case hits.length
  when 1 then nil
  when 0 then return [nil, "anchor no longer occurs in the file"]
  else        return [nil, "anchor is ambiguous (#{hits.length} matches)"]
  end

  region = region_at(body, hits.first)
  [fingerprint(region), "#{region.length} line(s) from :#{hits.first + 1}"]
end

# ── --record ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
if record
  head = `git -C #{Shellwords.escape(ROOT)} rev-parse --short HEAD 2>/dev/null`.strip
  head = "unknown" if head.empty?
  stamp = Time.now.strftime("%Y-%m-%d")

  out = +<<~HEAD
    # conformance-drift-baseline.tsv -- GENERATED by bin/check-conformance-drift --record.
    # Do not edit by hand.
    #
    # One `path<TAB>fingerprint<TAB>anchor` row per anchor in
    # spec/conformance-anchors.tsv. The fingerprint is the first 16 hex of
    # SHA-256 over the ANCHORED REGION -- the line the anchor sits on plus every
    # following line indented more deeply than it, which is the block that label
    # opens. `bin/check-conformance-drift` re-measures and lists what moved, so a
    # conformance sweep can re-judge the rows whose evidence changed instead of
    # sampling the matrix blind.
    #
    # THIS FILE IS A SWEEP'S WATERMARK, NOT A LOCK. Re-record it when a sweep has
    # RE-JUDGED the drifted rows -- never to make a run quiet. Nothing in CI reads
    # it; drift is the normal state between sweeps and a gate on it would have to
    # be switched off the day it was added.
    #
    # recorded: #{stamp} at #{head}#{label ? " -- #{label}" : ""}
    # #{pairs.length} anchors.
  HEAD

  missing = []
  pairs.sort.each do |rel, anchor|
    fp, why = measure(rel, anchor)
    if fp.nil?
      missing << "#{rel}: #{why} -- #{anchor.inspect}"
      next
    end
    out << "#{rel}\t#{fp}\t#{anchor}\n"
  end

  File.write(BASELINE, out)
  puts "wrote spec/#{File.basename(BASELINE)} (#{pairs.length - missing.length} of #{pairs.length} anchors, #{stamp} at #{head})"

  unless missing.empty?
    warn ""
    warn "check-conformance-drift: #{missing.length} anchor(s) could not be measured and are NOT in the baseline."
    warn "bin/check-conformance-anchors is the gate for this; it should be red too."
    missing.each { |m| warn "  x #{m}" }
    exit 1
  end
  exit 0
end

# ── the report ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
unless File.file?(BASELINE)
  warn "check-conformance-drift: no baseline at #{BASELINE}"
  warn "Record one with `bin/check-conformance-drift --record`; until then there is"
  warn "nothing to compare against and this cannot answer the question."
  exit 1
end

baseline = {}
recorded = nil
File.readlines(BASELINE, chomp: true).each do |line|
  recorded ||= line[/\A#\s*recorded:\s*(.+)\z/, 1]
  next if line.start_with?("#") || line.strip.empty?

  rel, fp, anchor = line.split("\t", 3)
  next if rel.nil? || fp.nil? || anchor.nil? || anchor.empty?

  baseline[[rel, anchor]] = fp
end

if baseline.empty?
  warn "check-conformance-drift: #{BASELINE} holds no fingerprints"
  exit 1
end

# ── optional: name the matrix rows, so the output is a work-list ─────────────
#
# The manifest carries no row ids -- deliberately: it is published in this repo
# and the matrix is not. Given the matrix, its rows are mapped onto the anchors
# here. The resolver mirrors the exporter's (`ks/` abbreviates kiosk-server's
# spec dir, a bare `e2e` means e2e/assistant.sh); citations into other repos are
# not in the manifest and are skipped.
PATH_TOKEN = %r{(?:ks/|e2e/|kiosk-[a-z0-9-]+/|kiosk\.tech/)[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9_./-]*\.(?:rb|sh|erb|rake|md|json|yml|yaml|html)}
ANCHOR_RE  = %r{@"((?:[^"\\]|\\.)*)"}
CITATION   = %r{(?<path>#{PATH_TOKEN}|(?<![A-Za-z0-9_/.-])e2e)(?<anchors>(?:#{ANCHOR_RE})+)}

rows_for = Hash.new { |h, k| h[k] = [] }
if matrix
  File.readlines(matrix, chomp: true).each do |line|
    row = line[/\A- (SPEC-\d+[a-z]?)/, 1]
    next if row.nil?

    line.scan(CITATION) do
      m = Regexp.last_match
      path = m[:path]
      next if path.start_with?("kiosk.tech/")

      rel = path == "e2e" ? "e2e/assistant.sh" : path.sub(%r{\Aks/}, "kiosk-server/spec/kiosk/server/")
      m[:anchors].scan(ANCHOR_RE) do
        anchor = Regexp.last_match(1).gsub('\\"', '"').gsub("\\\\", "\\")
        list = rows_for[[rel, anchor]]
        list << row unless list.include?(row)
      end
    end
  end
end

moved   = []
fresh   = []
broken  = []
same    = 0

pairs.sort.each do |rel, anchor|
  was = baseline[[rel, anchor]]
  fp, why = measure(rel, anchor)

  if fp.nil?
    broken << [rel, anchor, why]
  elsif was.nil?
    fresh << [rel, anchor, fp, why]
  elsif was == fp
    same += 1
  else
    moved << [rel, anchor, was, fp, why]
  end
end

retired = baseline.keys.reject { |k| pairs.include?(k) }

def label_for(rows_for, rel, anchor)
  rows = rows_for[[rel, anchor]]
  rows.empty? ? "" : "#{rows.join(', ')}  "
end

def report(title, entries, rows_for)
  return if entries.empty?

  puts
  puts title
  entries.group_by { |e| e[0] }.each do |rel, group|
    puts "  #{rel}"
    group.each do |entry|
      _rel, anchor, *rest = entry
      puts "    #{label_for(rows_for, rel, anchor)}#{anchor.inspect}"
      puts "      #{rest.compact.join('  ')}"
    end
  end
end

total = pairs.length
puts "check-conformance-drift: #{total} anchors, baseline recorded #{recorded || '(unstamped)'}"
puts "  unchanged #{same}   moved #{moved.length}   new #{fresh.length}   unmeasurable #{broken.length}" \
     "#{retired.empty? ? '' : "   retired #{retired.length}"}"
puts "  matrix rows named from #{matrix}" if matrix
puts "  (no --matrix given: anchors only, no SPEC row ids)" unless matrix

report("MOVED — the anchored region changed since the baseline; re-judge these rows:",
       moved.map { |rel, anchor, was, now, why| [rel, anchor, "#{was} -> #{now}", why] }, rows_for)
report("NEW — cited since the baseline was recorded, never judged against it:",
       fresh.map { |rel, anchor, fp, why| [rel, anchor, fp, why] }, rows_for)
report("UNMEASURABLE — bin/check-conformance-anchors is the gate for these:",
       broken.map { |rel, anchor, why| [rel, anchor, why, nil] }, rows_for)

unless retired.empty?
  puts
  puts "RETIRED — in the baseline, no longer cited by the matrix (informational):"
  retired.sort.each { |rel, anchor| puts "  #{rel}  #{anchor.inspect}" }
end

if moved.empty? && fresh.empty? && broken.empty?
  puts
  puts "No anchored region has moved. (This says where the text is unchanged. It does NOT"
  puts "say the tests still assert their rows — see the header for what this cannot see.)"
  exit 0
end

puts
puts "#{moved.length + fresh.length + broken.length} anchor(s) need re-judging. This is a TRIAGE LIST, not a failure:"
puts "walk them, then re-record the baseline with --record so the next sweep starts from here."
exit 2
